\section{Introduction}\label{sec:meta-circular-intro}

A \emph{meta-circular} definition is one that is expressed in the same language it defines. A meta-circular interpreter for Lisp is an interpreter for Lisp written in Lisp. A meta-circular compiler for a language $L$ is a compiler for $L$ written in $L$. This self-referential quality is not mere cleverness---it reveals deep structural properties of the language and its computational model.

The concept has its roots in McCarthy's original Lisp implementation (1960), where he demonstrated that the evaluation function for Lisp could be defined as a Lisp program. This was not a practical compiler---it was a definition, a mathematical specification of what Lisp means, expressed in Lisp itself. Yet it demonstrated something profound: a language can be powerful enough to describe its own semantics.

This chapter explores meta-circular definitions from interpreters through compilers to self-hosting systems. We examine the theoretical foundations, the practical techniques for bootstrapping, and the rich history of meta-circularity in languages from Lisp to ML to modern systems.

\section{What Is a Meta-Circular Definition?}\label{sec:meta-circular-def}

\subsection{The Concept of Circularity}

\begin{definition}[Meta-Circular Definition]\label{def:meta-circular}
A \emph{meta-circular definition} of a language $L$ is a program $P$ written in $L$ that defines the semantics of $L$. Formally, $P$ is a function such that for all programs $s \in L$ and inputs $d$:
\[
P(s, d) = \SemBrack{s}(d)
\]
where $\SemBrack{s}$ is the mathematical semantics of $s$.
\end{definition}

The circularity lies in the fact that $P$ is both the \emph{definiens} (the thing that does the defining) and a member of the \emph{definiendum} (the thing being defined). To understand $P$, one must already understand $L$; but $P$ is supposed to explain what $L$ means.

This apparent paradox is resolved by recognizing that the meta-circular definition is not a \emph{foundation} but a \emph{fixed point}. The semantics of $L$ are determined by the behavior of $P$ running on the machine that implements $L$. The circularity is \emph{well-founded} because the machine provides the base case: the hardware (or virtual machine) that executes the primitive operations.

\subsection{Fixed-Point Semantics of Meta-Circularity}

We can formalize the meta-circular relationship using fixed-point theory. Let $\Phi$ be the functional that maps an interpretation of $L$ to the interpretation defined by $P$:

\begin{equation}
\Phi(f)(s, d) = \text{the result of running } P \text{ on } (s, d) \text{ using } f \text{ as the primitive semantics}
\end{equation}

The meta-circular semantics is the \emph{least fixed point} of $\Phi$:
\begin{equation}
\SemBrack{-} = \mathit{lfp}(\Phi) = \bigsqcup_{n \geq 0} \Phi^n(\bot)
\end{equation}

where $\bot$ is the totally undefined semantics and $\Phi^n$ applies $\Phi$ iteratively. The fixed point exists by the Knaster--Tarski theorem (Theorem~\ref{thm:knaster-tarski}) because $\Phi$ is monotone on the complete lattice of partial functions.

\subsection{Meta-Circularity vs.\ Self-Interpretation}

A subtle but important distinction:

\begin{itemize}
  \item A \emph{meta-circular interpreter} is written in the language it interprets and uses the language's own constructs to define the semantics. It is a \emph{definition}.
  \item A \emph{self-interpreter} is a program that can interpret any program in its own language, including itself. It is a \emph{program} that happens to be written in the same language.
\end{itemize}

Every meta-circular interpreter is a self-interpreter (it can interpret itself), but not every self-interpreter is meta-circular in the strong sense. A self-interpreter might use external libraries, non-standard extensions, or implementation-specific features that are not part of the language being interpreted.

\section{Meta-Circular Interpreters}\label{sec:meta-circular-interpreters}

\subsection{McCarthy's Original Lisp Interpreter}

The first meta-circular interpreter was McCarthy's \texttt{eval} function for Lisp, published in 1960~\cite{mccarthy1960recursive}. Written in the notation of the lambda calculus with conditional expressions and recursive function definitions, it defined the semantics of Lisp in terms of itself:

\begin{equation}
\mathit{eval}(e, a) = \mathbf{case}\;e\;\mathbf{of}\begin{cases}
\mathit{atom}(e) & \Rightarrow \mathit{assoc}(e, a) \\
\mathit{atom}(\mathit{car}(e)) & \Rightarrow \mathbf{case}\;\mathit{car}(e)\;\mathbf{of}\;\begin{cases}
\texttt{quote} & \Rightarrow \mathit{cadr}(e) \\
\texttt{atom} & \Rightarrow \mathit{atom}(\mathit{eval}(\mathit{cadr}(e), a)) \\
\texttt{eq} & \Rightarrow \mathit{eval}(\mathit{cadr}(e), a) = \mathit{eval}(\mathit{caddr}(e), a) \\
\texttt{car} & \Rightarrow \mathit{car}(\mathit{eval}(\mathit{cadr}(e), a)) \\
\texttt{cdr} & \Rightarrow \mathit{cdr}(\mathit{eval}(\mathit{cadr}(e), a)) \\
\texttt{cons} & \Rightarrow \mathit{cons}(\mathit{eval}(\mathit{cadr}(e), a), \mathit{eval}(\mathit{caddr}(e), a)) \\
\texttt{cond} & \Rightarrow \mathit{evcon}(\mathit{cdr}(e), a)
\end{cases} \\
\text{otherwise} & \Rightarrow \mathit{eval}(\mathit{car}(e), a)\;\text{applied to}\;\mathit{evlis}(\mathit{cdr}(e), a)
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

This function is remarkable for several reasons:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item It is \emph{complete}: it defines the semantics of the entire language.
  \item It is \emph{executable}: with a few primitive operations provided by the machine, it can actually run.
  \item It is \emph{readable}: a programmer familiar with Lisp can understand it directly.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Modern Meta-Circular Interpreters}

The pattern established by McCarthy has been replicated in many languages:

\begin{example}[Meta-Circular Interpreter for a Simple Functional Language]\label{ex:meta-circular-fun}
\begin{verbatim}
  type expr =
    | Var of string
    | Lam of string * expr
    | App of expr * expr
    | Let of string * expr * expr
    | Int of int
    | Add of expr * expr
    | If of expr * expr * expr

  type value =
    | Closure of string * expr * (string -> value)
    | Num of int

  let rec eval env = function
    | Var x -> env x
    | Lam (x, body) -> Closure (x, body, env)
    | App (f, x) ->
        let Closure (param, body, closed_env) = eval env f in
        eval (fun y -> if y = param then eval env x else closed_env y) body
    | Let (x, e, body) -> eval (fun y -> if y = x then eval env e else env y) body
    | Int n -> Num n
    | Add (e1, e2) ->
        let (Num n1) = eval env e1 and (Num n2) = eval env e2 in Num (n1 + n2)
    | If (c, t, e) ->
        match eval env c with Num 0 -> eval env e | _ -> eval env t
\end{verbatim}
This interpreter is meta-circular if the host language supports the same constructs (closures, pattern matching, recursion) as the language being interpreted.
\end{example}

\subsection{Properties of Meta-Circular Interpreters}

\begin{theorem}[Self-Application of Meta-Circular Interpreters]\label{thm:self-interp}
Let $I$ be a meta-circular interpreter for language $L$, implemented in $L$ running on machine $M$. Then:
\[
I(I, s) = I(s)
\]
That is, the meta-circular interpreter can interpret itself.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $I$ is an interpreter for $L$, and $I$ itself is a program in $L$, $I$ can interpret $I$. The result of $I(I, s)$ is the result of running $I$ on $s$, which is $I(s)$.
\end{proof}

The proof is trivial, but the implications are profound. The self-application $I(I, s)$ involves two layers of interpretation: the outer $I$ interprets the inner $I$, which interprets $s$. Each layer adds overhead, so $I(I, s)$ is slower than $I(s)$. But it is \emph{correct}: it produces the same result.

\section{Meta-Circular Compilers}\label{sec:meta-circular-compilers}

\subsection{From Interpreter to Compiler}

A meta-circular \emph{compiler} is a compiler for $L$ written in $L$. Unlike a meta-circular interpreter, which directly computes results, a meta-circular compiler produces \emph{target code}---a program in some target language (which may be $L$ itself, or a lower-level language).

\begin{definition}[Meta-Circular Compiler]\label{def:meta-circular-compiler}
A \emph{meta-circular compiler} for language $L$ is a program $C$ written in $L$ such that for all source programs $s \in L$:
\[
C(s) = t \quad \text{where } t \text{ is target code and } \SemBrack{t} = \SemBrack{s}
\]
\end{definition}

The target code $t$ may be in the same language $L$ (a source-to-source transformation), in a lower-level language (compilation to machine code), or in an intermediate representation.

\subsection{A Meta-Circular Compiler for a Simple Language}

\begin{example}[Source-to-Source Compiler]\label{ex:source-to-source}
A simple compiler that translates a higher-level language to a lower-level one, both expressed in the same host language:
\begin{verbatim}
  (* Compile pattern matching to conditional branches *)
  let rec compile = function
    | Match (e, cases) ->
        let tmp = fresh_var () in
        Let (tmp, compile e, compile_cases (Var tmp) cases)
    | Lam (x, body) -> Lam (x, compile body)
    | App (f, x) -> App (compile f, compile x)
    | Let (x, e, body) -> Let (x, compile e, compile body)
    | e -> e  (* variables, constants *)
\end{verbatim}
This compiler is meta-circular if the host language has the same data types and control structures as the source language.
\end{example}

\subsection{Bootstrapping: The Compiler That Compiles Itself}

The most important application of meta-circularity is \emph{bootstrapping}: using a compiler to compile itself.

\begin{definition}[Bootstrapping]\label{def:bootstrapping}
\emph{Bootstrapping} is the process of using a compiler $C$ (written in language $L$ and running on machine $M$) to compile its own source code, producing a new version $C'$ that runs on $M$ without requiring the original $C$:
\[
C' = C(C_{\mathit{source}})
\]
After bootstrapping, $C'$ can compile any program in $L$, including itself, without the original $C$.
\end{definition}

