The bootstrapping process works as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Write a compiler $C_0$ for language $L$ in some existing language $M$ (e.g., C, assembly).
  \item Write the compiler's source code $C_{\mathit{source}}$ in $L$ itself.
  \item Use $C_0$ to compile $C_{\mathit{source}}$, producing $C_1$ (a version of the compiler that runs on $M$).
  \item Use $C_1$ to compile $C_{\mathit{source}}$ again, producing $C_2$.
  \item Verify that $C_1$ and $C_2$ behave identically. If they do, the compiler is \emph{bootstrapped}.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{theorem}[Bootstrapping Correctness]\label{thm:bootstrap-correct}
If $C_0$ is a correct compiler for $L$, and $C_1 = C_0(C_{\mathit{source}})$, then $C_1$ is also a correct compiler for $L$, and $C_1(C_{\mathit{source}}) = C_2$ is a correct compiler for $L$ that behaves identically to $C_1$ on all inputs.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $C_0$ is correct, $C_1$ correctly implements the compilation function for $L$. Since $C_1$ is a correct compiler written in $L$, compiling $C_{\mathit{source}}$ with $C_1$ produces $C_2$ that also correctly implements the compilation function. Since $C_1$ and $C_2$ implement the same function (the compilation function for $L$), they behave identically on all inputs.
\end{proof}

\section{Self-Hosting Compilers}\label{sec:self-hosting}

\subsection{What Does It Mean to Self-Host?}

A compiler is \emph{self-hosting} if it is written in the language it compiles. This is a stronger condition than meta-circularity: a self-hosting compiler must not only be written in $L$ but must be able to compile its own source code.

\begin{definition}[Self-Hosting Compiler]\label{def:self-hosting}
A compiler $C$ is \emph{self-hosting} if:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $C$ is written in language $L$.
  \item $C$ compiles programs written in $L$.
  \item $C$ can compile its own source code: $C(C_{\mathit{source}})$ produces a working compiler.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Significance of Self-Hosting}

Self-hosting is considered a milestone in the maturity of a programming language. It demonstrates that:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Expressiveness}: $L$ is expressive enough to implement a compiler---a complex, demanding application.
  \item \textbf{Performance}: $L$ is efficient enough that the compiled compiler is usable.
  \item \textbf{Completeness}: The language implementation is complete enough to support all features needed by a compiler.
  \item \textbf{Independence}: The language no longer depends on another language for its implementation.
\end{enumerate}

